WEBVTT

00:00:00.676 --> 00:00:02.696
Commas and FANBOYS.

00:00:02.696 --> 00:00:06.651
A comma plus a FANBOYS word joins two sentences.

00:00:06.651 --> 00:00:08.225
A comma alone never does.

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:11.587
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:16.382
The choices move commas around words like and, but, and so.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:18.547
Or around words like however and therefore.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.278
It's testing how to join two complete sentences.

00:00:23.314 --> 00:00:24.839
Here's the method.

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:28.994
Split the sentence at the joining word, and look at both sides.

00:00:28.994 --> 00:00:33.349
If there's a new subject after it, both sides are full sentences.

00:00:33.349 --> 00:00:36.149
Put a comma before the FANBOYS word.

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:39.909
If it's the same subject doing two things, no comma.

00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:44.309
And if the word is however, or therefore, it isn't FANBOYS.

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:47.093
Use a semicolon before it, and a comma after.

00:00:48.176 --> 00:00:50.841
Rule one: learn the FANBOYS.

00:00:50.841 --> 00:00:54.806
For, and, nor, but, or, yet, so.

00:00:54.806 --> 00:00:57.751
Only these seven can join two sentences with a comma.

00:00:58.839 --> 00:01:02.934
Rule two: one subject with two verbs gets no comma.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:05.963
There's no new subject after and, so there's no comma.

00:01:07.043 --> 00:01:10.753
Rule three: however is not a FANBOYS word.

00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:13.798
Commas alone around it make a comma splice.

00:01:13.798 --> 00:01:16.881
Use a semicolon before however, and a comma after.

00:01:17.893 --> 00:01:19.333
Let's try one.

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:21.733
The left side is a full sentence.

00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:26.223
The right side is another full sentence, so the two need a real break.

00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:31.793
However isn't a FANBOYS word, so commas alone can't join these sentences.

00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:33.728
That would be a comma splice.

00:01:33.728 --> 00:01:36.768
And no punctuation at all would be a run-on.

00:01:36.768 --> 00:01:40.623
We need a semicolon before however, and a comma after it.

00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:41.712
The answer is B.

00:01:42.773 --> 00:01:45.343
Watch out for a comma before and.

00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:48.918
If there's no new subject after it, there's no comma.

00:01:48.918 --> 00:01:52.103
The team practiced daily, and won the title.

00:01:52.103 --> 00:01:54.688
One subject, two verbs.

00:01:54.688 --> 00:01:55.333
No comma.

00:01:56.381 --> 00:02:03.041
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the rules, and the trap.

00:02:03.041 --> 00:02:05.861
